["<aside class=\"callout why\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>Your moose season near Heffley Creek is two weeks in November. Knowing what the moose did in the ten months before tells you where it stands on 1 November.</p></aside><figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 312\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Year wheel for moose in BC. Calves born late May and June. Antlers grow in velvet from late April or May to August and are full size by early September. The rut peaks in late September and early October. Antlers drop from mid November to March, old bulls first.\">\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"312\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"17\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">A moose year</text><circle cx=\"200\" cy=\"165\" r=\"108\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"200\" cy=\"165\" r=\"52\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\"/><path d=\"M265.6 237.8 A98 98 0 0 1 200.0 263.0\" stroke=\"#c9a77a\" stroke-width=\"16\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M103.2 180.3 A98 98 0 0 1 106.8 134.7\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"16\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M273.1 197.5 A80 80 0 0 1 137.8 215.3\" stroke=\"#3d6b3d\" stroke-width=\"10\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M143.4 108.4 A80 80 0 0 1 200.0 85.0\" stroke=\"#6b4f35\" stroke-width=\"10\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M200.0 85.0 A80 80 0 0 1 280.0 165.0\" stroke=\"#6b4f35\" stroke-width=\"10\" fill=\"none\"/><text x=\"232.09356159271258\" y=\"50.225197540155534\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">J</text><text x=\"287.6812408671319\" y=\"82.31875913286811\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">F</text><text x=\"319.77480245984447\" y=\"137.90643840728742\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">M</text><text x=\"319.77480245984447\" y=\"202.09356159271258\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">A</text><text x=\"287.6812408671319\" y=\"257.6812408671319\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">M</text><text x=\"232.09356159271258\" y=\"289.77480245984447\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">J</text><text x=\"167.90643840728742\" y=\"289.77480245984447\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">J</text><text x=\"112.31875913286811\" y=\"257.6812408671319\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">A</text><text x=\"80.22519754015555\" y=\"202.0935615927126\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">S</text><text x=\"80.22519754015553\" y=\"137.90643840728742\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">O</text><text x=\"112.31875913286808\" y=\"82.31875913286811\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">N</text><text x=\"167.90643840728745\" y=\"50.225197540155534\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">D</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"159\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Moose</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"177\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">year</text><text x=\"392\" y=\"46\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-weight=\"bold\">Rut: late Sep,</text><text x=\"392\" y=\"63\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-weight=\"bold\">early Oct</text><text x=\"8\" y=\"46\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#9a7a4a\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Calves: late</text><text x=\"8\" y=\"63\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#9a7a4a\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">May, June</text><text x=\"8\" y=\"290\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#3d6b3d\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Antlers grow:</text><text x=\"8\" y=\"307\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#3d6b3d\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">May to Aug</text><text x=\"392\" y=\"290\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-weight=\"bold\">Antlers drop:</text><text x=\"392\" y=\"307\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-weight=\"bold\">Nov to Mar</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Rut: late September and early October <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>Calves: late May and June <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: what this chapter covers</summary><ul><li>Month by month from January to December: antlers, rut, calving, growth, coat, ticks, movements and winter survival, then a close look at October and November.</li><li>Previous: <a href=\"#/s/mc-moose-04\">Chapter 4, The mind</a>. Next: <a href=\"#/s/mc-moose-06\">Chapter 6, The day</a>.</li></ul></details>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-moose-trail-snow.jpg\" alt=\"A moose trail ploughed through deep snow.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>A moose trail ploughed through deep snow. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: NPS staff, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moose_Browse_Collection,_Yukon-Charley_Rivers,_2003_2_(1482c7be-3b1f-42da-844e-544383bbd5da).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-moose-willows.jpg\" alt=\"Winter browse: willow tips bitten off.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Winter browse: willow tips bitten off. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Yellowstone National Park from Yellowstone NP, USA, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cow_moose_browsing_on_willows_(15951276174).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Twigs, deep snow, ticks feeding (85 to 90%).</li><li>Last antlers drop; yearlings keep theirs longest <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: January to March in detail</summary><ul><li>Food: up to 20 kg of twigs a day, mostly willow, plus red osier dogwood, cottonwood, paper birch, aspen, high bush cranberry, false box and subalpine fir; they also strip bark from willow and poplar (90%, BC account).</li><li>Where: dense browse along rivers, around wetlands, in burns and cutblocks, low avalanche tracks and the subalpine spruce willow birch zone <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>On plateaus they gather in winter along river valleys and in burns, logged areas and wetland complexes <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>Antlers: shed from mid November to March, older bulls first; \"by March only the yearlings may still have antlers\" <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>Winter ticks take their January blood meal; adult females engorge from March <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Deep crusted snow can starve moose and cut next year's calf survival (85%, Alaska Department of Fish and Game).</li></ul></details>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-moose-calf.jpg\" alt=\"Spring calf beside its mother.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Spring calf beside its mother. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: USFWSAlaska, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moose_calf_(50007803527).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-moose-cow-calf.jpg\" alt=\"Cow and calf: she defends it hard.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Cow and calf: she defends it hard. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: USFWS Mountain-Prairie, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shiras_Moose_Cow_and_Calf_Seedskadee_NWR_(15072335119).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Antlers start: late April or early May <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>Calves: late May and June, 11 to 16 kg <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: April to June in detail</summary><ul><li>Spring: once snow is patchy, many moose move quickly to spring ranges near their winter ranges (90%, BC account).</li><li>Ticks drop off in April and lay eggs <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>. Ghost moose are most visible March and April <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Calving: after about 8 months of gestation (230 days), in late May and June; pregnant cows seek seclusion and chase off last year's young (85 to 90%).</li><li>Single calves are the rule; 10 to 20% of BC cows have twins <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>. Twinning tracks food quality: under 10% in poor habitat up to 75% in good habitat in Alaska <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Calves stand on day one; cows often give birth lying down; calves start eating plants at 2 weeks <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>Most cows that die of poor condition in central BC die April to June (90%, BC moose research report).</li><li>Spring food: grazing as well as browsing; sedges, horsetail, pondweeds, grasses (85%, Alaska Department of Fish and Game).</li></ul></details>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-pondweed.jpg\" alt=\"Pondweed: moose dive for it in summer.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Pondweed: moose dive for it in summer. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Christian Fischer, CC BY-SA 3.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PotamogetonNatans.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/hab-willow-shrub.jpg\" alt=\"Willow leaves: summer salad.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Willow leaves: summer salad. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Matt Lavin from Bozeman, Montana, USA, CC BY-SA 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Salix_bebbiana_(5002370825).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Antlers grow fast in velvet <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>Summer food: water plants and leaves <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: July and August in detail</summary><ul><li>Antlers are \"the fastest growing tissue in any mammal\": a big Alaska bull can grow about an inch of antler a day (85%, Alaska Department of Fish and Game).</li><li>About 80% of antler growth is done by mid July (75%, Canadian Encyclopedia).</li><li>Summer food: horsetail, burweed and submerged pondweeds, plus new leaves of shrubs and herbs (90%, BC account).</li><li>Plateaus: summer moose spread out and are \"strongly attracted to lake shores, swamps, and Beaver ponds\" <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>Mountains: moose move to higher summer ranges; some stay in the valleys <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>Calves nurse; a cow gives at least 150 L of milk June to September <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>. Calves grow more than 10 times their birth weight in 5 months <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Bulls build fat over summer for the rut <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 290\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Timeline from April to March. Antlers start growing in late April or May and grow in velvet through August, reaching full size by early September. They are hard through the fall rut and are shed between mid November and March, old bulls first and yearlings last. Antlers keep growing until about age 10, with a spread up to 1.8 metres on a prime bull.\">\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"290\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"17\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">The antler year</text><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"200\" x2=\"380\" y2=\"200\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"44.0\" y=\"220\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">A</text><text x=\"73.5\" y=\"220\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">M</text><text x=\"103.0\" y=\"220\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">J</text><text x=\"132.5\" y=\"220\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">J</text><text x=\"162.0\" y=\"220\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">A</text><text x=\"191.5\" y=\"220\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">S</text><text x=\"221.0\" y=\"220\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">O</text><text x=\"250.5\" y=\"220\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">N</text><text x=\"280.0\" y=\"220\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">D</text><text x=\"309.5\" y=\"220\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">J</text><text x=\"339.0\" y=\"220\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">F</text><text x=\"368.5\" y=\"220\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">M</text><rect x=\"44\" y=\"120\" width=\"140\" height=\"30\" fill=\"#7a5a3a\" rx=\"4\"/><text x=\"114\" y=\"140\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">grow in velvet</text><rect x=\"184\" y=\"120\" width=\"60\" height=\"30\" fill=\"#e9dcc0\" stroke=\"#23261f\" rx=\"4\"/><text x=\"214\" y=\"140\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">hard</text><rect x=\"244\" y=\"160\" width=\"120\" height=\"30\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" rx=\"4\"/><text x=\"304\" y=\"180\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">shed</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Full size by early September</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"87\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Old bulls shed first; yearlings last</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"248\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Antlers keep growing to about age 10</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"265\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Spread up to 1.8 m on a prime bull</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Antlers full size by early September <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>Velvet dries and is rubbed off <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: September in detail</summary><ul><li>BC account <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>: \"In British Columbia, Moose antlers reach full size by early September. Then the velvet dries, and the Moose rubs it off against trees or shrubs.\"</li><li>Bulls rub saplings and brush; the rut pits start (75%, chapter 8).</li><li>Mid September to mid October: winter tick larvae climb on <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Moose breed from September to November in BC <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>The Region 3 general moose seasons in some MUs (Management Units) open 20 September; not near Heffley Creek (99%, chapter 10).</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 290\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Timeline September to December. The first heat in late September and early October, about two weeks, conceives more than 80 percent of calves. Cows that miss come into a second and third heat about 25 days apart. The MU 3-27 spike fork season, 1 to 15 November, comes after the main rut.\">\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"290\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"17\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Rut timing in BC</text><line x1=\"30\" y1=\"150\" x2=\"380\" y2=\"150\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"40\" y1=\"142\" x2=\"40\" y2=\"158\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"40\" y=\"176\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Sep 1</text><line x1=\"150\" y1=\"142\" x2=\"150\" y2=\"158\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"150\" y=\"176\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Oct 1</text><line x1=\"260\" y1=\"142\" x2=\"260\" y2=\"158\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"260\" y=\"176\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Nov 1</text><line x1=\"370\" y1=\"142\" x2=\"370\" y2=\"158\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"370\" y=\"176\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Dec 1</text><rect x=\"113\" y=\"96\" width=\"50\" height=\"40\" fill=\"#e8590c\" rx=\"4\"/><text x=\"138\" y=\"70\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">1st heat</text><text x=\"138\" y=\"121\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">80%</text><rect x=\"190\" y=\"110\" width=\"30\" height=\"26\" fill=\"#f0a070\" rx=\"4\"/><text x=\"205\" y=\"100\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">2nd</text><rect x=\"246\" y=\"118\" width=\"26\" height=\"18\" fill=\"#f6c9a6\" rx=\"4\"/><text x=\"259\" y=\"112\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">3rd</text><rect x=\"260\" y=\"196\" width=\"18\" height=\"24\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" rx=\"3\"/><text x=\"252\" y=\"214\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-weight=\"bold\">MU 3-27: Nov 1 to 15</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"250\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">80% of calves conceived in 2 weeks,</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"267\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">late Sep to early Oct. Then every 25 days.</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Over 80% of calves are conceived in 2 weeks <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>Late September to early October <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li></ul><a class=\"yt-card\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=jdTg-LOokes\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"><span class=\"yt-play\">▶</span><span class=\"yt-text\"><b>Moose Calling Techniques - How To (Eastmans' Hunting)</b><small>Hear real moose calls and when to use each one.<br>Eastmans Hunting Journals, YouTube, 5 min. Needs internet.</small></span></a><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: the rut in detail</summary><ul><li>BC account <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>: \"more than 80 percent of calves are conceived during the first estrus period (heat), which occurs during two weeks in late September and early October. Cows that do not conceive during the first estrus have second or third estrus periods at about 25 day intervals.\"</li><li>Cows give a long quavering moan audible up to 3 km <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>\"Several cows often gather around a prime bull, or two fighting bulls, and wallow in the urine soaked rutting pits the bulls have made.\" Bulls do not form a harem <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>Alaska: rut peak late September and early October; the winner usually mates with several cows <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>The <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"Rut\">Rut</button> in other words: elk first, then moose, then deer (80%, <a href=\"#/s/the-rut\">the rut</a>).</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 296\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Two columns. October: the rut winds down, bulls are tired and lean, moose are still high near lakes and swamps, leaves drop and twigs take over the menu. November: bulls feed hard, old bulls start dropping antlers from mid November, snow drifts moose down slowly, and the MU 3-27 season runs 1 to 15 November. Moose come down last, after deer and elk.\">\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"296\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"17\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">October and November</text><rect x=\"14\" y=\"40\" width=\"182\" height=\"220\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><rect x=\"204\" y=\"40\" width=\"182\" height=\"220\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#e3e8ef\" stroke=\"#4a7fb0\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"105\" y=\"66\" font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">October</text><text x=\"295\" y=\"66\" font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#4a7fb0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">November</text><text x=\"24\" y=\"96\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\">Rut winds down</text><text x=\"24\" y=\"122\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\">Bulls tired, lean</text><text x=\"24\" y=\"148\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\">Still high, near</text><text x=\"24\" y=\"174\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\">lakes and swamps</text><text x=\"24\" y=\"200\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\">Leaves drop:</text><text x=\"24\" y=\"226\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\">twigs on the menu</text><text x=\"214\" y=\"96\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\">Bulls feed hard</text><text x=\"214\" y=\"122\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\">Old bulls start</text><text x=\"214\" y=\"148\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\">dropping antlers</text><text x=\"214\" y=\"174\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\">Snow drifts them</text><text x=\"214\" y=\"200\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\">down, slowly</text><text x=\"214\" y=\"226\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"start\">Your 1 to 15 Nov</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"286\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Moose come down last, after deer and elk</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Bulls tired and lean by late October <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Moose stay high longer than deer and elk <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: October in detail</summary><ul><li>Second heat: cows that missed in early October cycle again about 25 days later, so some rut activity carries into late October (90%, BC account; my reading of the timing).</li><li>\"By late October, adult males have exhausted their summer accumulation of fat and their desire for female company. Once again they begin feeding.\" (85%, Alaska Department of Fish and Game)</li><li>Moose \"are able to stay on high elevation ranges much longer than Elk, deer, or mountain sheep\" (90%, BC account).</li><li>Leaves drop; the menu turns to twigs <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Tick larvae take their first blood meal <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 475\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Season bar chart: Region 3 moose and bighorn sheep. Moose ✻ spike fork bulls: 3-34 to 3-38, 3-40 to 3-44, 3-46: Sept 20 to Oct 31. Moose ✻ spike fork bulls: 3-15 to 3-17, 3-31 to 3-33: Oct 15 to Nov 15. Moose ✻ spike fork bulls: 3-12 to 3-14, 3-18 to 3-20, 3-26 to 3-30, 3-39: Nov 1 to Nov 15. Sheep, full curl bighorn rams: 3-31, +3-32: Sept 10 to Oct 20. + Map C21 closure. Sheep, mature bighorn rams: ✻3-17: Sept 10 to Oct 20. ✻ Map C2 only.\" font-family=\"sans-serif\">\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"475\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"26\" font-size=\"17\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Region 3 moose and bighorn sheep</text>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"47\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Page 34. Bag 1 each.</text>\n<text x=\"74.0\" y=\"83\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Sep</text>\n<text x=\"200.0\" y=\"83\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Oct</text>\n<text x=\"326.0\" y=\"83\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Nov</text>\n<line x1=\"12.0\" y1=\"91\" x2=\"12.0\" y2=\"465\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-opacity=\"0.6\"/>\n<line x1=\"136.0\" y1=\"91\" x2=\"136.0\" y2=\"465\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-opacity=\"0.6\"/>\n<line x1=\"264.0\" y1=\"91\" x2=\"264.0\" y2=\"465\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-opacity=\"0.6\"/>\n<line x1=\"388.0\" y1=\"91\" x2=\"388.0\" y2=\"465\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-opacity=\"0.6\"/>\n<text x=\"12\" y=\"107\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\">Moose ✻ spike fork bulls: 3-34 to 3-38, 3-40</text>\n<text x=\"12\" y=\"125\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\">to 3-44, 3-46</text>\n<rect x=\"90.5\" y=\"131\" width=\"173.5\" height=\"22\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/>\n<text x=\"177.3\" y=\"147\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#ffffff\" text-anchor=\"middle\">Sept 20 to Oct 31</text>\n<text x=\"12\" y=\"181\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\">Moose ✻ spike fork bulls: 3-15 to 3-17, 3-31</text>\n<text x=\"12\" y=\"199\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\">to 3-33</text>\n<rect x=\"193.8\" y=\"205\" width=\"132.2\" height=\"22\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/>\n<text x=\"187.8\" y=\"221\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"end\">Oct 15 to Nov 15</text>\n<text x=\"12\" y=\"255\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\">Moose ✻ spike fork bulls: 3-12 to 3-14, 3-18</text>\n<text x=\"12\" y=\"273\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\">to 3-20, 3-26 to 3-30, 3-39</text>\n<rect x=\"264.0\" y=\"279\" width=\"62.0\" height=\"22\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\"/>\n<text x=\"258.0\" y=\"295\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"end\">Nov 1 to Nov 15</text>\n<text x=\"12\" y=\"329\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\">Sheep, full curl bighorn rams: 3-31, +3-32</text>\n<rect x=\"49.2\" y=\"335\" width=\"169.4\" height=\"22\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n<text x=\"224.6\" y=\"351\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\">Sept 10 to Oct 20</text>\n<text x=\"12\" y=\"375\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#e8590c\">+ Map C21 closure</text>\n<text x=\"12\" y=\"407\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\">Sheep, mature bighorn rams: ✻3-17</text>\n<rect x=\"49.2\" y=\"413\" width=\"169.4\" height=\"22\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\"/>\n<text x=\"224.6\" y=\"429\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#23261f\">Sept 10 to Oct 20</text>\n<text x=\"12\" y=\"453\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#e8590c\">✻ Map C2 only</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><aside class=\"callout warn\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Warning</div><p>Study aid only. The official regulations are the law.</p></aside><ul><li>MUs (Management Units) 3-27 and 3-28: spike fork bulls, 1 to 15 November <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span>.</li><li>Mature bulls start shedding antlers from mid November <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: November in detail</summary><ul><li>Seasons: page 34, Region 3; MUs 3-12 to 3-14, 3-18 to 3-20, 3-26 to 3-30 and 3-39, spike fork bulls, 1 to 15 November, bag 1 (99%, <a href=\"#/s/rb-region-3\">Region 3</a>). Region 8 rows: 1 to 15 November too (99%, <a href=\"#/s/rb-region-8\">Region 8</a>).</li><li>A third heat about 25 days after the second may still bring a little rut behaviour in early November (75%, my reading of the 25 day cycles).</li><li>Antler shedding: \"between mid November and March. The older bulls shed earliest\" (90%, BC account). Alaska: mature bulls shed as early as November, mostly December and January <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>. A big bull may already be bald by late November; your legal spike fork usually keeps his antlers (85%, my reading).</li><li>Moose return to valley bottoms gradually; with light snow they may stay at mid elevation until late winter (90%, BC account).</li><li>Deer rut starts mid November: you share the woods with busy deer hunters (80%, <a href=\"#/s/the-rut\">the rut</a>).</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 298\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Snow layers beside a moose and a deer. Moose move easily in snow up to 40 centimetres, with only slight difficulty at 40 to 70 centimetres, and often winter where snow is 50 centimetres or more. Deer have trouble past about 30 centimetres.\">\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"298\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"17\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Long legs, deep snow</text><rect x=\"20\" y=\"210\" width=\"360\" height=\"40\" fill=\"#eef3f8\"/><rect x=\"20\" y=\"160\" width=\"360\" height=\"50\" fill=\"#dde8f2\"/><rect x=\"20\" y=\"110\" width=\"360\" height=\"50\" fill=\"#c9daea\"/><g transform=\"translate(105 192) scale(0.95)\"><rect x=\"-36\" y=\"4\" width=\"8\" height=\"52\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#9c8a76\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"-26\" y=\"4\" width=\"8\" height=\"52\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#9c8a76\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"26\" y=\"4\" width=\"8\" height=\"52\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#9c8a76\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><rect x=\"36\" y=\"4\" width=\"8\" height=\"52\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#9c8a76\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/><path d=\"M-46 0 Q-48 -26 -20 -26 Q10 -40 34 -34 Q48 -26 44 2 Q20 22 -30 18 Q-48 16 -46 0 Z\" fill=\"#3b2a1c\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M30 -30 L56 -40 L66 -26 L40 0 Z\" fill=\"#3b2a1c\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><path d=\"M56 -44 L86 -30 Q92 -22 84 -18 L64 -20 Q54 -26 56 -44 Z\" fill=\"#3b2a1c\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/><path d=\"M66 -22 Q66 -6 60 -4 Q58 -12 62 -22 Z\" fill=\"#3b2a1c\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><ellipse cx=\"54\" cy=\"-50\" rx=\"8\" ry=\"3.5\" transform=\"rotate(-30 54 -50)\" fill=\"#3b2a1c\" stroke=\"#23261f\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><circle cx=\"70\" cy=\"-34\" r=\"1.8\" fill=\"#eeeeee\"/></g><text x=\"390\" y=\"246\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-weight=\"bold\">to 40 cm: easy</text><text x=\"390\" y=\"196\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-weight=\"bold\">40 to 70 cm: slight trouble</text><text x=\"390\" y=\"146\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-weight=\"bold\">often winters in 50 cm plus</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"272\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Long legs: moose own deep snow</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"290\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#8b5a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Deer: trouble past about 30 cm</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li>Snow drives moose to winter range, slowly <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>Bulls feeding to rebuild before deep winter <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: December in detail</summary><ul><li>Winter ranges: dense browse along rivers, wetlands, burns, logged areas (90%, BC account). The Hunt Map has a Moose winter range layer (99%, chapter 7).</li><li>Most of the year's antlers drop December and January in Alaska <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li><li>Moose are hit on roads and rail lines every winter, especially along the Yellowhead corridor (90%, BC account).</li><li>Tick larvae go dormant until January <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul></details>","<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Month</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Jan to Mar</td><td>Twigs, deep snow, ticks feed, last antlers drop (85 to 90%)</td></tr><tr><td>Apr to Jun</td><td>Antlers start, ticks drop, calves born (85 to 90%)</td></tr><tr><td>Jul to Aug</td><td>Antlers in velvet, water plants <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Sep</td><td>Velvet off, rut starts <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Late Sep to early Oct</td><td>Peak rut <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Oct</td><td>Rut fades, bulls feed <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></td></tr><tr><td>Nov</td><td>Your season; old bulls shed (90 to 99%)</td></tr><tr><td>Dec</td><td>Down to winter range <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: why this matters for a November hunter</summary><ul><li>Your 1 to 15 November bull has finished the main rut, is lean, feeding hard and likely still high or mid slope (85%, my reading of the sources above).</li><li>Rut calling is a September and October game; in November, think food and cover (Tip, chapter 9).</li></ul></details>","<figure class=\"diagram\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 312\" font-family=\"sans-serif\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Year wheel for moose in BC. Calves born late May and June. Antlers grow in velvet from late April or May to August and are full size by early September. The rut peaks in late September and early October. Antlers drop from mid November to March, old bulls first.\">\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"400\" height=\"312\" fill=\"#fffaf0\"/>\n<text x=\"200\" y=\"24\" font-size=\"17\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">A moose year</text><circle cx=\"200\" cy=\"165\" r=\"108\" fill=\"#dfe6d3\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"200\" cy=\"165\" r=\"52\" fill=\"#fffaf0\" stroke=\"#8a9a6b\"/><path d=\"M265.6 237.8 A98 98 0 0 1 200.0 263.0\" stroke=\"#c9a77a\" stroke-width=\"16\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M103.2 180.3 A98 98 0 0 1 106.8 134.7\" stroke=\"#e8590c\" stroke-width=\"16\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M273.1 197.5 A80 80 0 0 1 137.8 215.3\" stroke=\"#3d6b3d\" stroke-width=\"10\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M143.4 108.4 A80 80 0 0 1 200.0 85.0\" stroke=\"#6b4f35\" stroke-width=\"10\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M200.0 85.0 A80 80 0 0 1 280.0 165.0\" stroke=\"#6b4f35\" stroke-width=\"10\" fill=\"none\"/><text x=\"232.09356159271258\" y=\"50.225197540155534\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">J</text><text x=\"287.6812408671319\" y=\"82.31875913286811\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">F</text><text x=\"319.77480245984447\" y=\"137.90643840728742\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">M</text><text x=\"319.77480245984447\" y=\"202.09356159271258\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">A</text><text x=\"287.6812408671319\" y=\"257.6812408671319\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">M</text><text x=\"232.09356159271258\" y=\"289.77480245984447\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">J</text><text x=\"167.90643840728742\" y=\"289.77480245984447\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">J</text><text x=\"112.31875913286811\" y=\"257.6812408671319\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">A</text><text x=\"80.22519754015555\" y=\"202.0935615927126\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">S</text><text x=\"80.22519754015553\" y=\"137.90643840728742\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">O</text><text x=\"112.31875913286808\" y=\"82.31875913286811\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">N</text><text x=\"167.90643840728745\" y=\"50.225197540155534\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#23261f\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">D</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"159\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">Moose</text><text x=\"200\" y=\"177\" font-size=\"15\" fill=\"#2f3a2b\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-weight=\"bold\">year</text><text x=\"392\" y=\"46\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-weight=\"bold\">Rut: late Sep,</text><text x=\"392\" y=\"63\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#e8590c\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-weight=\"bold\">early Oct</text><text x=\"8\" y=\"46\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#9a7a4a\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Calves: late</text><text x=\"8\" y=\"63\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#9a7a4a\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">May, June</text><text x=\"8\" y=\"290\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#3d6b3d\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">Antlers grow:</text><text x=\"8\" y=\"307\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#3d6b3d\" text-anchor=\"start\" font-weight=\"bold\">May to Aug</text><text x=\"392\" y=\"290\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-weight=\"bold\">Antlers drop:</text><text x=\"392\" y=\"307\" font-size=\"14\" fill=\"#6b4f35\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-weight=\"bold\">Nov to Mar</text>\n</svg>\n</figure><ul><li><strong>Rhyme:</strong> \"Velvet in summer, rubbed in September; rut till October, dropped by December.\"</li><li><strong>Number trick:</strong> 2, 80, 25: in 2 weeks, 80% of calves conceived; misses cycle every 25 days <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li><strong>Old hand's rule</strong> (tradition): \"Moose come down last.\"</li></ul><details class=\"more\"><summary>More: more hooks</summary><ul><li><strong>Rhyme:</strong> \"Calves in June, ticks in fall, ghosts in spring, antlers fall.\"</li><li><strong>Number trick:</strong> 230: days of gestation, about 8 months (85 to 90%).</li><li><strong>Number trick:</strong> 11 to 16, then 135 to 180: calf kilograms at birth, then by early winter <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li><strong>Old hand's rule</strong> (widely reported, opinion): \"The calendar calls the rut; the cold calls the moose down.\"</li></ul><aside class=\"callout mistake\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Expecting a rut on 10 November in MU 3-27: the main rut is a month earlier.</li><li>Following the deer down to the low country in early November.</li><li>Thinking a bald bull in late November must be a cow: mature bulls shed early.</li></ul></aside><aside class=\"callout field\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Do this in the field</div><p>Put three dates in your phone: 25 September (rut peak, scout and listen), 1 November (season opens), 31 March (Mandatory Hunter Report).</p></aside></details>","<ul><li>BC Ministry of Environment, Lands and Parks (2000), <a href=\"https://www.env.gov.bc.ca/wld/documents/moose.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">\"Moose in British Columbia\"</a>, read directly, checked 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span></li><li>Alaska Department of Fish and Game, <a href=\"https://www.adfg.alaska.gov/static/education/wns/moose.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moose notebook</a> and <a href=\"https://www.adfg.alaska.gov/index.cfm?adfg=moose.printerfriendly\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">species profile</a>, read directly, checked 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></li><li>BC, <a href=\"https://www.bcsf.org/s/Latest_moose_winter_tick_program_report.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Provincial Moose Winter Tick Surveillance Program 2021</a>, read directly, checked 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span></li><li>BC Ministry of Forests (2023), <a href=\"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/assets/gov/environment/plants-animals-and-ecosystems/wildlife-wildlife-habitat/moose/2022_moose_research_progress_report.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">moose research progress report, May 2022</a>, read directly, checked 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span></li><li>The Canadian Encyclopedia, <a href=\"https://www.thecanadianencyclopedia.ca/en/article/moose\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moose</a>, checked 2026-10-11 <span class=\"cert c-lo\" title=\"Certainty\">75%</span></li><li>BC Hunting and Trapping Regulations Synopsis 2026 to 2028, pages 34 and 68, via <a href=\"#/s/rb-region-3\">Region 3</a> and <a href=\"#/s/rb-region-8\">Region 8</a> <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span></li><li>Research notes: <code>research/2026-10-11-masterclass-moose.md</code>.</li></ul>"]